# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
descartes-discourse-4.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1 and descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1.2 have "rarely_new"; descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1.3 has "proof_judgments → rarely_new" and "rarely_new → critics_worse"; they differ when only "I have already had frequent proof of the judgments of friends, of indifferent persons, and of malicious and envious ones" and "it has rarely happened that anything was objected to me which I had myself altogether overlooked, unless far removed from the subject" are true, which the first allows
descartes-discourse-4.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "I have already had frequent proof of the judgments of friends, of indifferent persons, and of malicious and envious ones" and "it has rarely happened that anything was objected to me which I had myself altogether overlooked, unless far removed from the subject" are true and "every critic of my opinions has appeared to me either less rigorous or less equitable than myself" is false. Is S4, "But it has rarely happened that anything has been objected to me which I had myself altogether overlooked, unless it were something far removed from the subject: so that I have never met with a single critic of my opinions who did not appear to me either less rigorous or less equitable than myself.", true then?
descartes-discourse-4.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1 has "strive_victory" and "¬advocates_judges"; descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1.2 and descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1.3 have "¬disputation_truth" and "strive_victory" and "¬advocates_judges"; they differ when only "some truth before unknown has been brought to light by the disputations practised in the schools" and "in the disputations each strives for victory and is more occupied with mere verisimilitude than with weighing the reasons on both sides" are true, which the first allows
descartes-discourse-4.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered yes, which keeps descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1, to: Suppose "some truth before unknown has been brought to light by the disputations practised in the schools" and "in the disputations each strives for victory and is more occupied with mere verisimilitude than with weighing the reasons on both sides" are true and "those who have long been good advocates are on that account afterwards the better judges" is false. Is S5, "And further, I have never observed that any truth before unknown has been brought to light by the disputations that are practised in the schools; for while each strives for the victory, each is much more occupied in making the best of mere verisimilitude, than in weighing the reasons on both sides of the question; and those who have been long good advocates are not afterwards on that account the better judges.", true then?
descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1 has "rarely_new ∧ critics_worse ∧ ¬disputation_truth → ¬expect_profit" and "opp_useful → expect_profit"; descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1.2 has "rarely_new → critics_worse" and "strive_victory ∧ ¬advocates_judges → ¬disputation_truth" and "proof_judgments ∧ critics_worse ∧ ¬disputation_truth → ¬expect_profit"; they differ when only "these oppositions would be useful, both in showing me my errors and in bringing others to understand and add to my views", "I anticipate profit from objections to my views", "I have already had frequent proof of the judgments of friends, of indifferent persons, and of malicious and envious ones", "it has rarely happened that anything was objected to me which I had myself altogether overlooked, unless far removed from the subject", "every critic of my opinions has appeared to me either less rigorous or less equitable than myself", "some truth before unknown has been brought to light by the disputations practised in the schools" and "in the disputations each strives for victory and is more occupied with mere verisimilitude than with weighing the reasons on both sides" are true, which the first allows; descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1.3 has "critics_worse ∧ ¬disputation_truth → ¬expect_profit"
descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1 takes for granted "if these oppositions would be useful, both in showing me my errors and in bringing others to understand and add to my views, then I anticipate profit from objections to my views"; descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1.2 and descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1.3 do not [in 1 of 2 runs]
descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered no, which keeps descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1.2 and descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if these oppositions would be useful, both in showing me my errors and in bringing others to understand and add to my views, then I anticipate profit from objections to my views"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument. [in 1 of 2 runs]
descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: U2: descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1.2 and descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1.3 take for granted "if it has rarely happened that anything was objected to me which I had myself altogether overlooked, unless far removed from the subject, then every critic of my opinions has appeared to me either less rigorous or less equitable than myself"; descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1 does not [in 1 of 2 runs]
descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1.2 and descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if it has rarely happened that anything was objected to me which I had myself altogether overlooked, unless far removed from the subject, then every critic of my opinions has appeared to me either less rigorous or less equitable than myself"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument. [in 1 of 2 runs]
descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: U3: descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1.2 and descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1.3 take for granted "if (in the disputations each strives for victory and is more occupied with mere verisimilitude than with weighing the reasons on both sides and it is not so that those who have long been good advocates are on that account afterwards the better judges), then it is not so that some truth before unknown has been brought to light by the disputations practised in the schools"; descartes-discourse-4.logic.r1 does not [in 1 of 2 runs]
